**Serious findings remain.** The revised plan improves the earlier checks, but its required-question gold can still miss a question that matching loses.

1. **The required-question gold cannot be derived completely from the pair labels.** [Plan lines 106–108](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:106) define G1 as comparisons with already-open questions, then claim G2′ can be derived from candidates labelled “a question of its own.” But “a question of its own” is not one of G1’s three listed labels. A candidate with no earlier question to compare has no pair label at all. In the [v38 ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/english-v38-nvda-1w-2026-10-28/views.sqlite), match record 2 has two candidates and says `no list` for both. Same-answer candidates can also be coalesced by [identity.py lines 51–63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/src/hivemind/views/identity.py:51). A regression that loses one can therefore escape the stated G2′ check. The distinction between matching losses and questions extraction never proposed is now correctly stated.

2. **The false-merge guard can be waived by the control.** [Plan lines 132 and 141](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:132) require zero false merges, but also say a threshold the control fails against itself is not used. If that includes the zero-false-merge threshold, an arm could pass after merging away a required question. The working rule says a control that fails its own bar does not make that bar informative; it does not establish that a lost question is acceptable ([AGENTS.md lines 299–301](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:299)). The plan needs to say how individual losses remain a stop condition when the control is noisy.

