**Serious findings remain.** For example, the revised gold can still miss a required successor question, and it can also demand that a distinct but trivial question be opened.

1. **Serious — the required-question check excludes successors.** [Plan lines 107–109]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:107) give “successor to X” its own G1 label, but derive G2′ only from candidates labelled “a question of its own.” A required later-period question can therefore disappear without failing G2′. The sealed ledger’s `v68`, the 2027 CPU-revenue successor to `v24`, is a concrete case; the [earlier review, line 92]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:92) identifies it. The [standard, line 317]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:317) treats the next period as a successor.

2. **Serious — “distinct” is being equated with “must open.”** [Plan lines 107–109]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:107) give every candidate an identity label but no “distinct, yet not worth opening” label; G2′ then makes every “question of its own” mandatory. The [standard, lines 281–287]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:281), separately requires worth and independent investment meaning. For example, the earlier reader marked the distinct RTX 50 SUPER release question `v19` as “do not open” ([gold, line 25]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:25)); that label is not Alex-approved, but it shows the distinction the gold must be able to express. As written, G1, G2 and G2′ can disagree.

3. **Serious — the proposed holdout includes cases already read.** [Plan line 115]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:115) uses the sealed run’s second half as the holdout. Its first 50 questions were already labelled in the earlier review, including `v48` on August 4 and `v49` on August 7 in the stored ledger; the [earlier gold, lines 54–55]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:54) records those labels. The earlier review also discusses `v68` on August 28 ([line 92]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:92)). Blind readers help, but a date split alone does not make these cases unseen. [AGENTS.md, lines 246–255]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/AGENTS.md:246) requires a held-out set no arm has seen.

4. **Serious — the evidence guard still omits successor evidence.** [Plan line 135]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:135) checks evidence for **merged** candidates. It does not check evidence for a newly opened successor. The sealed run’s `v68` was opened without the evidence that prompted it, as documented in the [earlier review, lines 90–92]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/report.md:90). That loss can pass the stated check. The revised expected-destination check is an improvement for merges.

5. **Serious — H2’s key future transition is not exercised by the stated replay.** [Plan lines 94 and 176]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:94) propose opening a later question when the earlier deadline expires. The stored Kyber example’s earlier deadline is December 31, 2027, while the sealed replay ends in August 2026. The [planned measurements]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:142) do not state a check that advances through that expiry. A replay could pass while the deferred successor never opens.

